  <model metaid="meta_simvastatin_intestine" id="simvastatin_intestine" name="Model for intestinal/gut simvastatin metabolism" substanceUnits="mmole" timeUnits="min" volumeUnits="litre" areaUnits="m2" lengthUnits="metre" extentUnits="mmole">
    <notes>
      <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <h1>Model for intestinal/gut simvastatin metabolism</h1>
        <ul>
          <li>Simvastatin (lactone) is absorbed from the intestinal lumen into the
enterocytes, the fraction <code>F_abs_simva</code> of it; the rest is excreted in
the feces.</li>
          <li>Simvastatin is metabolized by CYP3A4 (and CYP3A5) in the enterocytes
(first pass metabolism) or exported into the gut plasma (portal vein).
The intestinal availability is about 0.25 - 0.3: regular grapefruit
juice, an inhibitor of the intestinal CYP3A4 only, increases the AUC of
simvastatin 3.6-fold (Lilja2004).</li>
          <li>The simvastatin metabolites from the bile (enterohepatic circulation)
are absorbed with the fraction <code>F_abs_simmets</code>, the rest is excreted in
the feces (only metabolites and unabsorbed simvastatin are in the feces).</li>
          <li>The hydrolysis of simvastatin to simvastatin acid (esterases) and the
metabolism of simvastatin acid take place in the liver, not in the
intestine.</li>
          <li>Food does not change the bioavailability of simvastatin (Zocor label),
the model has no food effect.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The content of this model has been carefully created in a manual research effort.</p>
